./crates/printer/src/color.rs:96:/// A single color specification provided by the user.
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:97:///
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:98:/// ## Format
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:99:///
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:100:/// The format of a `Spec` is a triple: `{type}:{attribute}:{value}`. Each
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:101:/// component is defined as follows:
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:102:///
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:103:/// * `{type}` can be one of `path`, `line`, `column`, `match` or `highlight`.
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:104:/// * `{attribute}` can be one of `fg`, `bg` or `style`. `{attribute}` may also
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:105:///   be the special value `none`, in which case, `{value}` can be omitted.
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:106:/// * `{value}` is either a color name (for `fg`/`bg`) or a style instruction.
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:107:///
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:108:/// `{type}` controls which part of the output should be styled.
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:109:///
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:110:/// When `{attribute}` is `none`, then this should cause any existing style
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:111:/// settings to be cleared for the specified `type`.
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:112:///
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:113:/// `{value}` should be a color when `{attribute}` is `fg` or `bg`, or it
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:114:/// should be a style instruction when `{attribute}` is `style`. When
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:115:/// `{attribute}` is `none`, `{value}` must be omitted.
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:116:///
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:117:/// Valid colors are `black`, `blue`, `green`, `red`, `cyan`, `magenta`,
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:118:/// `yellow`, `white`. Extended colors can also be specified, and are formatted
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:119:/// as `x` (for 256-bit colors) or `x,x,x` (for 24-bit true color), where
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:120:/// `x` is a number between 0 and 255 inclusive. `x` may be given as a normal
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:121:/// decimal number of a hexadecimal number, where the latter is prefixed by
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:122:/// `0x`.
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:123:///
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:124:/// Valid style instructions are `nobold`, `bold`, `intense`, `nointense`,
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:125:/// `underline`, `nounderline`, `italic`, `noitalic`.
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:126:///
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:127:/// ## Example
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:128:///
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:129:/// The standard way to build a `UserColorSpec` is to parse it from a string.
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:130:/// Once multiple `UserColorSpec`s have been constructed, they can be provided
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:131:/// to the standard printer where they will automatically be applied to the
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:132:/// output.
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:133:///
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:134:/// A `UserColorSpec` can also be converted to a `termcolor::ColorSpec`:
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:135:///
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:136:/// ```rust
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:137:/// # fn main() {
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:138:/// use termcolor::{Color, ColorSpec};
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:139:/// use grep_printer::UserColorSpec;
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:140:///
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:141:/// let user_spec1: UserColorSpec = "path:fg:blue".parse().unwrap();
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:142:/// let user_spec2: UserColorSpec = "match:bg:0xff,0x7f,0x00".parse().unwrap();
./crates/printer/src/color.rs:143:///
